I. INTRODUCTION
THE concept of autonomous vehicles (AVs) has been in focus for several years and a great deal of progress has been made recently [1]. Some robotaxis, such as DiDi [2] and Waymo [3], can be seen in some cities, such as Shanghai and San Francisco. However, current AVs still have limitations, such as computational efficiency and low robustness [4]. They can encounter difficulties in unexpected scenarios, including public events and roadworks. To address these challenges, teleoperation emerges as an effective intervention method for AVs when they are unable to handle such situations. Therefore, it can be an effective method to bridge the gap between Level 4 and 5 AVs [5], and to accelerate the commercialisation of AVs. Generally, teleoperation modes are categorized into remote monitoring, remote assistance, and remote driving [6]. In remote monitoring mode, operators solely act as observers, without exerting direct influence on the performance of the dynamic driving task. Remote assistance mode comes into play when AVs confront situations outside their operating design domain and require advice. On the other hand, remote driving mode necessitates remote drivers to assume full control of the vehicle using steering, throttle, and brake pedals. The remote driving mode would be the focus of the study in this article, as it presents additional challenges due to the altered driving environment in a remote driving station. Furthermore, there are various challenges when it comes to driving behaviour and experience of remote drivers, since they rely on transmitted video and audio, which introduce latency and reduced resolution compared to real-life driving. Consequently, corresponding risks can emerge, such as distraction or inattention of remote drivers [7]. To establish clearer liability standards of remote driving in real traffic, the UK Law Commission recommends that drivers should bear the same responsibility as conventional drivers, except for faults beyond their control, such as loss of connection [8].
A literature review reveals numerous obstacles faced by remote driving. Firstly, there is a latency between the real vehicle and the remote control tower when transmitting the signal [9]. This latency, which is a combination of sensor latency, network latency, actuator latency, etc., could decrease driving performance in comparison with driving in a real car. Second, remote drivers’ ability to perceive the surrounding environment could also be reduced due to the decreased quality of the view compared to the view in a real car [10].

