Furthermore, remote drivers will experience different motion cues and audio information compared to real-life driving. This could potentially impact drivers’ ability to accurately judge vehicle states, such as acceleration and speed. As a result, driving behaviour and experience can be affected.
The driving behaviour in teleoperation is closely linked to both motion comfort and safety. According to Papaioannou et al. [11], the behaviour of the steering, particularly the steering velocity, can significantly influence the level of motion sickness and comfort experienced by passengers in a remote driving car. Similarly, research by Zhao et al. [12] indicates that steering force feedback in teleoperation can impact the steering behaviour. This suggests that the design of the driving station plays a vital role in influencing driving behaviour, which in turn affects the comfort of passengers and their susceptibility to motion sickness. Additionally, Zhao et al. also discover that teleoperation leads to an increase in lane-following deviation, which can be attributed to specific characteristics of the steering force feedback, such as steering returnability. This has potential implications for driving safety during teleoperation. Hence, there is a critical need to conduct comprehensive studies on driving behaviour and its implications in teleoperation.
The driving experience has the potential to significantly influence both driving performance and behaviour. Gómez et al. [13], [14] discover a correlation between a driver’s experience and behaviour metrics, such as the relationship between steering feel and vehicle handling. This correlation provides insights into how the driving experience, specifically steering feedback, influences behaviours in remote driving. Additionally, Almallah et al. [15] find that an enhanced feeling of presence through improved display resolution could reduce simulator sickness. Similarly, Hosseini and Lienkamp [16] utilize head-mounted displays (HMDs) to enhance the telepresence feeling of remote drivers, leading to improved driving performance and reduced workload for these drivers. Furthermore, studies [17], [18] demonstrate that a driver’s emotional state significantly impacts driving performance, where feelings of anger and happiness can lead to risky driving behaviours. These findings underscore the necessity of enhancing the driving experience during remote driving. Although some of these studies are conducted on driving simulators, they offer valuable insights applicable to teleoperation due to similar driving station settings.
The driving behaviour and experience can be related to the fidelity of the driving station [19]. The design of it in teleoperation can share some similarities with driving simulator studies, such as interface design and driving feedback. Therefore, driving simulator studies can offer valuable insights into how driving behaviour and experience are influenced by the driving station fidelity in teleoperation. Several driving simulator studies have explored the impacts of simulator fidelity on driver behaviour and experience, particularly regarding sound and motion-cueing feedback. For the sound feedback, Merat and Jamson [20] find that without auditory cues, drivers struggle to maintain a specific speed and tend to travel faster than the required speed. Hellier et al. [21] reach similar conclusions, observing that drivers with no or low auditory feedback tend

to drive faster. Furthermore, Bringoux et al. [22] discover that synthesizing sound to evoke motion dynamics, such as pitch variation, has a greater impact on drivers’ behaviour during emergency braking compared to traditional sound.
For the motion-cueing feedback, driving simulator studies in [23] and [24] demonstrate that full motion cues can assist drivers in braking more smoothly before reaching a target stop point, in contrast to situations without motion cues. Wang et al. [25] further investigate the impact of motion cues on driving performance on highway roads, revealing their significant influence on speed control while navigating up-grade roads. Moreover, drivers provided with motion-cueing feedback report experiencing less discomfort and tiredness. However, the study in [26] suggests that motion cues do not have a significant and substantial effect on the performance of curve negotiation tasks.
Numerous driving simulator studies have examined the influence of motion-cueing and sound feedback on drivers. However, most of these studies have been conducted individually, and only a few have directly compared the two feedback types in practical teleoperation scenarios. Understanding the differences in the effects of these feedback modes on drivers’ behaviour and experience is crucial in the context of teleoperation. Such understanding can serve as a valuable reference for designing remote driving stations and avoiding redundant feedback modes in cases where similarities exist between them. Therefore, a comparative study is essential to gain relevant insights for remote driving.
This paper presents a virtual teleoperation study conducted using a driving simulator to investigate the influence of different driving feedback modes on driving behaviour and experience. The study includes four driving feedback modes: No feedback (NF), Motion-cueing feedback (MF), Sound and vibration feedback (SF), and a combination of motion-cueing, sound and vibration feedback (CF).
This study is guided by the following research objectives (ROs):
RO 1: Investigate the impact of different types of driving feedback modes on driving behaviour and experience in a low-speed disturbance scenario.
RO 2: Examine how different types of driving feedback modes influence driving performance during dynamic driving scenarios.
RO 3: Explore the relationship between drivers’ driving experience and their driving behaviour.
The remaining part of this paper is organised as follows. The simulator setup and feedback design, such as teleoperation prototype, motion-cueing feedback, and sound and vibration feedback, are described in Section II. Section III presents the experimental method. The results and discussion are presented in Section IV. Finally, Section V presents the conclusions and discusses future work.
