B. Vehicle Motion Feedback
The motion feedback of the vehicle is realised on the small size six DOF hexapod platform. RCTDS focuses on providing remote operators with information about the real vehicle states and surrounding environments, such as yaw rate, speed and road surface feeling, etc. Compared with existing remote driving stations, RCTDS intends to replicate the driving experience of a passenger car so that it can be used in real scenarios. For example, it can replicate both the low-frequency vehicle motion cues and high-frequency vibrations from road surface, such as cobblestone and gravel. To enhance the road surface feeling, RCTDS simulates the

vibration data of a real vehicle that are recorded from the real application scenarios, such as cobblestone. Although the size of the motion platform is small, it can generate almost the same roll and pitch angular rates as a real vehicle, and almost half of the yaw rate in normal operation. In contrast, due to the limited motion space of the simulator, the linear acceleration components Ax, Ay and Az are not used in the motion cues. However, the tilt angles transformed from accelerations are instead used to help remote operators sense the acceleration [27].
For the angular rate control of the driving simulator, the integration and washout filter-based methods are used separately. Traditionally, roll rate and pitch rate are not as large as yaw rate. The roll and pitch angles in the simulator are directly generated by integrating the pitch and roll rates of the vehicle signal, as shown in (1), which can provide almost the same roll and the pitch rates as the real vehicle.

