III. EXPERIMENT METHOD
A. Experiment Design of Scenario 1
1) Driving Scenario Design: The main research purpose of Scenario 1 is to investigate the influence of feedback types on driving behaviour and experience in a low-speed scenario (30 km/h) with road disturbances, similar to situations encountered on campuses or within communities. As shown in Fig. 4(a), disturbances include speed bumps, broken road, cobblestones, and manholes. Fig. 4(b) shows the view of the participants on the cobblestone road. The within-subjects design method is used with four feedback modes including No Feedback (NF), Motion-cueing Feedback (MF), Sound and vibration Feedback (SF), and Combination of motion cueing, sound and vibration Feedback (CF). In addition, each disturbance is added twice in the scenario, appearing in different sequences, to enhance the driving experience. A 30 km/h speed limit sign is also placed on the surface of the road, and the physical speed limit of the vehicle is set to 40 km/h. The entire scenario can be completed in 5 minutes.
2) Participants: There are 30 participants (20 male and 10 female) in the Scenario 1 test, and all of them are students and research engineers at KTH. The average age of the participants is 26.4 years (SD = 3.59). All participants have a passenger-car driving licence.
3) Questionnaire Design: To address the research objective of investigating how driving feedback influences driving experience, a written questionnaire is compiled (Table I).

