B. Experiment Design of Scenario 2
1) Driving Scenario Design: The main purpose of Scenario 2 is to investigate the influence of driving feedback on driving behaviour in a dynamic driving scenario with higher speed compared to Scenario 1. This is because this scenario tries to simulate the dynamic driving tasks on city roads. Therefore, the objective assessment is used. To evaluate this, three manoeuvres are used, including slalom, double lane change (DLC), and centre lane keeping (CLK), as shown in Fig. 5. For the slalom, the drivers are supposed to cross five cones spaced 25 metres apart. For the DLC, the ISO3888-1 is used, which is an international standard on designing the DLC manoeuvre for testing the vehicle dynamic metrics of road vehicles, while an additional 0.5-metre width is added between the cones to provide a better view of the cones’ positions. For the CLK, drivers are instructed to keep the car in the centre of the road. Cornering manoeuvres are then added between the slalom and DLC manoeuvres to simulate normal driving situations. In addition, the within-subjects design method is also used with the four driving feedback modes. It takes 3-4 mins for drivers to drive one lap.
2) Participants: Of the 30 participants from the Scenario 1 experiment, 22 participants join the Scenario 2 experiment (16 male and 6 female). The time interval between participation of the drivers in Scenario 1 and Scenario 2 experiments is at least 10 days. In addition, the participants also practise about six minutes in a condensed version of Scenario 2, which needs about two minutes for each round. The average age of the drivers is 27.64 years (SD = 4.24). The other information concerning the drivers is the same as that of Scenario 1.
3) Experiment Protocol: Due to the different research focus of Scenario 2, the instructions to drivers are also different from those of Scenario 1. To investigate driving performance, drivers are told to keep the car in the centre of the road and as close to the cones as possible without hitting them when driving through the slalom. In DLC, the drivers should keep the car in the centre between the cones. For speed control, drivers are required to drive as fast as they feel comfortable and feel they could perform the tasks safely. The physical speed limit of the vehicle is set to 60 km/h and is conveyed verbally to participants instead of being displayed on the road surface, given its uncommon occurrence in city road settings. In the formal test, drivers only drive one lap in each driving feedback mode due to the longer driving distance of this scenario compared to Scenario 1. The other experimental settings are the same as those of Scenario 1.

C. Statistical Methods

Data analysis begins with foundational descriptive statistics, such as the mean and standard deviation, to elucidate the primary trends within the dataset. Following this, the statistics Toolbox in Matlab (2020b) is used for statistical analysis. A one-way repeated measures analysis of variance (ANOVA) is performed to assess the significance among the feedback modes. Data within the 10th and 90th percentiles are utilized for each group to mitigate the influence of outliers [31]. To compare the effect size among groups, the partial eta squared (η2) criterion is calculated [32]. A η2 value of 0.01 indicates a small effect, 0.06 indicates a medium effect, and 0.14 indicates a large effect. The related F value, P value, and effect size are reported in the following section. Subsequently, post-hoc comparisons are conducted between each group to identify differences between pairs of variables. For these pairwise comparisons, the t-test is employed. In order to reduce the Type I error rate during the multiple t-test comparisons, the Bonferroni crrection method [33] is used in this paper. Therefore, statistically significant results are evaluated at the αc = 0.0083 level, which is calculated as

