a) Overall assessment: Fig. 6(a) shows the distribution of the overall assessment under different feedback modes, which include No Feedback (NF), Motion-cueing Feedback (MF), Sound and vibration Feedback (SF), and Combination of Motion-cueing, sound and vibration Feedback (CF). It shows that CF (M = 3.98, S D = 0.57) gets the highest rating, followed by MF (M = 3.65, S D = 0.70); SF (M = 3.32, S D = 0.47) comes in third and NF (M = 2.69, S D = 0.70) gets the lowest rating, where M and S D denote the mean value and standard deviation respectively. This trend indicates that CF is the most suitable feedback mode from the perspective of drivers. NF is, as expected, the worst case during driving due to little feedback information. In addition, the significant main effect is also found among these feedback modes (F(3,188) = 33.84, p < 0.001, η2 = 0.37).
b) Realistic: The results of realistic feeling show a trend similar to that of the overall assessment (F(3,188) = 54.35, p < 0.001, η2 = 0.48), as shown in Fig. 6(b). The results show that CF (M = 3.78, S D = 0.42) could provide drivers with the best realistic feeling and that NF (M = 1.95, S D = 0.55) is the least realistic. Unlike the results of the overall assessment, the difference between MF (M = 3.00, S D = 0.81) and SF (M = 3.09, S D = 0.80) is not significant ( p = 0.0725). This suggests that drivers have a similar realistic feeling in MF and SF modes.
