C. Comparative Analysis
To compare drivers’ preferences for driving feedback modes in Scenarios 1 and 2, the final rankings for each mode are determined by asking participants to assess the feedback modes after each test (Fig. 12). In this ranking, 1st indicates the most preferred and 4th indicates the least preferred. Significant differences in preferences are observed for various feedback modes in both Scenario 1 (F(3,116) = 135.56, p < 0.001, η2 = 0.78) and Scenario 2 (F(3,84) = 39.82, p < 0.001, η2 = 0.58). The CF (M = 3.87, S D = 0.43) receives the highest preference score, while NF (M = 1.07, S D = 0.37) receives the lowest preference score in

Scenario 1. The similar pattern is observed in Scenario 2, but the order of MF and SF differs between the two scenarios. MF (M = 2.62, S D = 0.62) is assessed higher than SF (M = 2.43, S D = 0.63) in Scenario 1, while the opposite is observed in Scenario 2. This difference is possibly influenced by the road design in each scenario. In Scenario 1, drivers rely more on motion-cueing feedback to control the vehicle, especially when facing disturbances. On the other hand, in Scenario 2, where there are no disturbances on the road, drivers use sound and vibration feedback more to gauge vehicle speed states. This could explain why drivers prefer SF in Scenario 2.
Additionally, the results in Scenarios 1 and 2 demonstrate that the advantages of driving feedback modes vary when evaluated from different perspectives, such as assessment methods and scenarios. From the subjective assessment in Scenario 1, CF is comprehensively rated higher than other feedback modes, as it provides drivers with a better driving experience, including a realistic feeling and improved perception of speed, among other factors. Similarly, participants also rate CF as the best in Scenario 2. From an objective assessment perspective, MF and CF demonstrate similar effects in Scenario 1. For instance, drivers with MF and CF find it easier to slow down before a speed bump compared to those with NF and SF. However, MF and CF may lead to delayed reaction times due to actuator latency in motion-cueing feedback. Hence, while CF enhances driving experience and performance in low-speed scenarios, it may introduce disadvantages in dynamic driving scenarios due to the latency in motion-cueing feedback.
V. CONCLUSION AND FUTURE WORK
A comparative study is conducted to investigate the impact of driving feedback on driving behaviour and experience in a virtual teleoperation experiment. Regarding RO 1, it is found that Motion-cueing feedback, when combined with sound and vibration feedback, significantly improve the driving experience in low-speed disturbance scenarios, leading to better subjective feelings. Moreover, it enhanced drivers’ attention to throttle and speed control, particularly when encountering road disturbances like speed bumps. In relation

