to RO 2, it is observed that motion-cueing feedback results in longer reaction times during the slalom manoeuvre due to actuator latency. This underscores a potential limitation of motion-cueing feedback in the context of teleoperation. For RO 3, the study reveals that road surface sensing can influence the TRR metric. Additionally, it demonstrates that sound and vibration feedback outperform motion-cueing feedback in terms of speed perception from the perspective subjective assessment in Scenario 1.
In future research, it would be beneficial to explore speed- and scenario-based adaptive amplitude motion-cueing feedback to provide remote operators with relevant motion information when needed. Additionally, the integration of vehicle dynamic information, such as disturbances and grip level, with audio and other feedback to remote operators could be worth investigating. In the next step, the real-life teleoperation experiments with various driving feedback modes will be conducted. Finally, it is essential to systematically study the influence of actuator latency on remote operators’ reaction time and consider potential methods to overcome it.
Limitations of the study: There are two main limitations in this study. Firstly, although the experiment is conducted using a virtual teleoperation platform with efforts to maximize the driving simulator’s realism, differences still remain between the simulator and real-life teleoperation. For example, the sound feedback used in this experiment is the simulated sound of the engine and wind. In real-life teleoperation, the sound from the surrounding environments and tyres could also be provided. Secondly, the questionnaire employed in Scenario 1 is not considered in Scenario 2. Several potential research points could still be explored based on this. For example, the influence of drivers’ feeling on driving accuracy and lateral dynamic control. Therefore, a future study could be done based on the following two aspects: first, the real-life teleoperation experiment will be designed to explore the influences of different driving feedback, and second, to explore the influences of driving feeling on drivers’ lateral dynamic control and performance.
